OUTPUT STANDARDIZATION — EXPORTS, COPY, AND HANDOFFS

\================================================================================



Export and copy behavior

\- Every export names the artifact clearly (product,
project or context, date or version) — no generic “export.txt.”

\- Export actions are explicit buttons with plain
labels: “Export PDF,” “Copy to clipboard,” “Save encrypted backup” — not
ambiguous “Share.”

\- Success feedback: short confirmation line or toast;
failure: plain reason + what to fix — same tone as validation errors.

\- Clipboard copy of secrets or tokens auto-clears
after a short timeout when the platform supports it; user sees a one-line
notice.



Handoff outputs

\- When the app prepares data for an external site or
tool, offer the same output shapes where possible: structured file (JSON/CSV),
human-readable sheet, and copy-friendly plain text.

\- Handoff screens state what the app produced, what
the user must paste or upload elsewhere, and what was not sent over the
network.

\- Readiness summary before handoff: checklist or
bullet list tied to engine state — not a static marketing blurb.





\================================================================================

FOOTERS, LEGAL LINKS, AND SUPPORT PLACEMENT

\================================================================================



Where Privacy, Terms, and Support live (fixed
contract)

\- Every app exposes the same five user-facing support
entry points with the same labels:

  Help |
Feedback | Support center | Privacy | Terms

\- Placement rules:

  - Desktop:
application menu (or Help menu) — always reachable from the main window.

  - Mobile:
support row, drawer section, or settings support block — visible without
completing a workflow.

  - Feedback
page footer: repeat links to Privacy, Terms, Support center, and Contact — so
legal and help are discoverable from the send flow itself.

\- URLs follow one site pattern per product:

  -
Privacy:  
https\://nivo78.com/{product}/privacy/

  - Terms:     https\://nivo78.com/{product}/terms/

  -
Support:  
https\://nivo78.com/{product}/support/ 
(knowledge base / help center)

  -
Contact:   mailto or support page linked
from Feedback — never a hidden address buried in About only.

\- In-app About / Data security (where the product has
one) links to the same Privacy URL — no duplicate policy text that can drift.



In-app and website linking standard (APP-WEBSITE-LINKS-STANDARD)

\- Every app provides clean, non-pushy pathways to the product website and support center:

  1. Support menu: exact 5 canonical items (Help, Feedback, Support center, Privacy, Terms) — Support center opens https\://nivo78.com/{product}/support/ in the system browser. After those five items, Help chrome MUST also include **Check for updates** immediately before **About** (`APP-SUPPORT-CHECK-FOR-UPDATES`).

  2. In-app Help hub (F1): window action bar provides an "Online guides & support (nivo78.com)" link leading to https\://nivo78.com/{product}/support/.

  3. About dialog / screen: prominent, clickable "Visit https\://nivo78.com/{product}/" link alongside product version, local-first promise, philosophy tagline, and copyright.

\- Constraint: Never nag, spam pop-ups, toast prompts, or interruptive review/visit prompts. Links are discoverable in standard menu and help chrome only. **Check for updates** is user-initiated only (no startup/timer background checks).

In-app store update check (`APP-SUPPORT-CHECK-FOR-UPDATES`) [QA]

\- Every store-distributed Nivo78 app MUST expose **Check for updates** in Help chrome (desktop: Help ▾; mobile: Help / Settings / Support equivalent).

\- Placement: immediately before **About**, after the five canonical support items.

\- Behavior on store installs: up to date; update available (store install and/or open listing); cannot determine (neutral message + optional open listing). Use official store APIs — not a custom version server in shipped products.

\- NOT APPLICABLE only when release docs state there is no store channel (rare).



Footer content (when a page has a footer)

\- Utility footers are minimal: legal links, app
version (optional), data-on-device reminder on first-run or security pages only
— not copyright walls on every screen.

\- Workflow footers hold Save/Cancel or Next/Back —
never mix legal links into the save bar; legal links live in menu + Feedback
footer.



App shell footer (desktop and tablet/shared shell —
every Nivo78 app that shows a window footer)

\- Two centered lines, same order and jobs in every
product:

  1. Philosophy
only: “Local-first · No Nivo78 account · No cloud sync · No analytics”

     (adapt only
when the product’s job explicitly requires an account or optional sync — state
the exception plainly).

     Do NOT
append publisher city on this line.

  2. Copyright
with place of business: “© {year} Nivo78 Mobile Apps, LLC · Tyler, Texas. All
rights reserved.”

\- About / credits dialogs that show copyright MUST use
the same copyright line (include Tyler, Texas there — do not duplicate a
separate “LLC · Tyler” line above it).





\================================================================================

FEEDBACK, BUG REPORT, SUPPORT, AND CONTACT — ONE
INTERFACE CONTRACT

\================================================================================



Single Feedback destination per app

\- One in-app Feedback page (not separate “Contact us”
and “Report bug” silos unless they are tabs on the same page).

\- Report type chips or tabs — same three types in
every app:

  -
Feedback      — comments, praise, or
general product notes (not how-to)

  - Bug
report    — something broke or looks
wrong

  - Feature
request — idea for a future version (honest: not a commitment)

\- How-to questions belong in the in-app Help hub /
Support center — NOT a Feedback report type.

\- Contact block on every send:

  - Name
(required)

  - Email
(required, validated)

  - Phone
(optional)

  - Pre-fill
name and email from profile when the product stores them; user edits before
send.

\- Subject line: sensible default per report type; user
can edit.

\- Narrative fields depend on type:

  - Bug: What
were you doing? / What happened? / What did you expect? / Additional details
(optional)

  - Feedback:
Message (comments)

  - Feature:
Describe the feature

\- Send path: compose mailto (or platform mail handoff)
— user reviews the draft in their mail app and taps Send. The app never
silently transmits.

\- Primary send action label: **Send request** (N78-Ops
UiCopy.Feedback.sendButton pattern). Not “Submit”, “Send email”, or “Review in
mail app” alone.



Email draft body — structured sections
(APP-FEEDBACK-BUG-NARRATIVE) [GATE]

\- Canonical reference: N78-Ops FeedbackScreen.kt,
SupportFeedbackRouting, UiCopyFeedback. Family utilities implement the same
contract scaled to product data. Ops diagnostics detail:
docs/FEEDBACK_DATA_COLLECTION.md.

\- Contact block at the top of the mailto body (before
report-type narrative):

  - NAME: {trimmed name}

  - EMAIL: {normalized email}

  - PHONE: {formatted phone or N/A}

\- Report narrative MUST use these exact delimiter lines
(support tooling parses them):

  - --- REPORT_TYPE --- (human-readable type label on
the next line)

  - Feedback: --- FEEDBACK ---

  - Bug report: --- WHAT_WERE_YOU_DOING --- / ---
WHAT_HAPPENED --- / --- WHAT_DID_YOU_EXPECT --- / --- ADDITIONAL_DETAILS ---
(only when the user entered optional additional comments)

  - Feature request: --- FEATURE_REQUEST ---

\- Blank narrative fields use the placeholder **(not
provided)**.

\- Always append on every report type: ---
SETTINGS_FOR_SUPPORT --- (minimal operational snapshot — app version, device/OS
summary, active screen, aggregate non-PII counts per product policy; never
business record contents).

\- Extended diagnostics (Bug report only when checkbox
ON, default ON; optional on Feedback comments default OFF; never Feature
request): --- EXTENDED_DIAGNOSTICS --- (product-specific redacted appendix).

\- Forbidden for Bug report: a single unstructured
“Details” or “Message” field instead of separate doing / happened / expected
fields; omitting --- SETTINGS_FOR_SUPPORT ---; silent transmit without OS mail
review.



Support center vs Feedback

\- Support center opens the hosted knowledge base in
the browser (offline-safe: show a clear message if network unavailable).

\- Feedback is for messages to Nivo78; Support center
is for self-serve articles — cross-link both directions in intro copy.



Contact without Feedback

\- Products may expose “Email support” as a secondary
action with the same contact defaults — still user-initiated mailto, still no
silent send.



Email routing (same three report types + two general
channels)

\- feedback\@nivo78.com — typed reports from in-app
Feedback (Feedback / Bug report / Feature request); subject prefix per type
({Product} Feedback:, Bug report:, Feature request:).

\- support\@nivo78.com — licensing, account, and channel
when the user skips structured Feedback (not a Feedback chip).

\- contact\@nivo78.com — business, press, partnerships —
not product bug triage.





